        <div class="section" id="n2-1">
            <h2><span class="section-icon">🧵</span>2.1 Lines and cables</h2>
<p>A line or cable is characterised by four <b>per-length</b> quantities (per km): resistance <i>r</i>, inductance <i>l</i> (reactance
<i>x</i> = <i>l</i>ω), capacitance <i>c</i> and conductance <i>g</i> (neglected). Orders of magnitude:</p>
<table>
<tr><th>Asset</th><th>r (Ω/km)</th><th>x (Ω/km)</th><th>c (nF/km)</th><th>Remark</th></tr>
<tr><td>MV overhead line, AAAC 148 mm²</td><td class="num">0.22 to 0.27</td><td class="num">0.35 to 0.40</td><td class="num">9 to 10</td><td>R/X ≈ 0.7</td></tr>
<tr><td>MV overhead line, AAAC 54.6 mm²</td><td class="num">0.60</td><td class="num">0.40</td><td class="num">9</td><td>R/X ≈ 1.5</td></tr>
<tr><td>MV underground cable, Al 150 mm²</td><td class="num">0.21</td><td class="num">0.11</td><td class="num">250 to 400</td><td>large capacitance</td></tr>
<tr><td>LV copper cable 4 × 95 mm²</td><td class="num">0.24</td><td class="num">0.08</td><td class="num">—</td><td>R/X ≈ 3</td></tr>
</table>
<p>Models according to the electrical length: <b>short line</b> (&lt; 80 km overhead): <i>R</i> + j<i>X</i> in series; <b>medium line</b> (<b>π</b>
model): <i>R</i> + j<i>X</i> in series and <i>C</i>/2 to earth at each end; long line: distributed-parameter model. NRJXpert offers the R, L, RL
and RLC π models (<b>Network</b> palette) with <i>R</i> = <i>r·ℓ</i>, <i>L</i> = <i>l·ℓ</i>, <i>C</i> = <i>c·ℓ</i>.</p>
<p><b>Zero-sequence quantities.</b> For earth faults, <i>r</i>₀, <i>x</i>₀ (return through the earth, about 3 times <i>x</i> for overhead lines) and
<i>c</i>₀ are also needed: « Zero sequence » section of the lines.</p>

<!-- ================================================================== -->
            <a href="#top" class="back-to-top">↑ Back to top</a>
        </div>
        <div class="section" id="n2-2">
            <h2><span class="section-icon">🔸</span>2.2 Transformers</h2>
<p>The Kapp equivalent circuit reduces the transformer to a series impedance <i>Z</i><sub>sc</sub> = <i>R</i><sub>sc</sub> + j<i>X</i><sub>sc</sub> and a
magnetising branch (<i>R</i><sub>fe</sub> ∥ j<i>X</i><sub>m</sub>), from the short-circuit test (<i>u</i><sub>sc</sub>, <i>P</i><sub>sc</sub>) and the
no-load test (<i>i</i>₀, <i>P</i>₀):</p>
<div class="eq">Z<sub>sc</sub> = u<sub>sc</sub>·U²/S<sub>n</sub> &nbsp;&nbsp; R<sub>sc</sub> = P<sub>sc</sub>·U²/S<sub>n</sub>² &nbsp;&nbsp; X<sub>sc</sub> = √(Z<sub>sc</sub>² − R<sub>sc</sub>²)
&nbsp;&nbsp; (U = voltage of the side to which the impedance is referred)</div>
<p>The <b>vector group</b> (Dyn, YNd, Yyn…) plays no role in the positive sequence but determines the zero-sequence path: a delta winding blocks
the zero-sequence current, an earthed star neutral lets it through. Dyn11, very common in distribution, isolates the MV network from LV earth
faults and provides the LV network with an earthed neutral.</p>
<div class="ex"><h4>Example 2.1 — Distribution transformer</h4>
<p>250 kVA, 30 kV / 400 V, <i>u</i><sub>sc</sub> = 4 %, <i>P</i><sub>sc</sub> = 3.25 kW. Calculate <i>Z</i><sub>sc</sub>, <i>R</i><sub>sc</sub>, <i>X</i><sub>sc</sub>
referred to the secondary, the rated current and the voltage drop at full load with cos φ = 0.8.</p>
<details class="sol"><summary>Show the solution</summary>
<p><i>Z</i><sub>sc</sub> = 0.04 × 400²/250 000 = <span class="result">25.6 mΩ</span>; <i>R</i><sub>sc</sub> = 3 250 × 400²/250 000² = <span class="result">8.32 mΩ</span>;
<i>X</i><sub>sc</sub> = <span class="result">24.21 mΩ</span>. <i>I</i><sub>n</sub> = 250 000/(√3 × 400) = 360.8 A.</p>
<p>ΔU ≈ √3·<i>I</i>·(<i>R</i> cos φ + <i>X</i> sin φ) = √3 × 360.8 × (0.00832 × 0.8 + 0.02421 × 0.6) = <span class="result">13.2 V, i.e. 3.3 %</span>.</p>
<p>These are exactly the values computed by the parameter dialog of the NRJXpert three-phase transformer (Network palette).</p>
</details></div>

<!-- ================================================================== -->
            <a href="#top" class="back-to-top">↑ Back to top</a>
        </div>
        <div class="section" id="n2-3">
            <h2><span class="section-icon">📗</span>2.3 Per-unit system</h2>
<p>A base power <i>S</i><sub>b</sub> (common to the whole network, e.g. 100 MVA or 10 MVA) and a base voltage <i>U</i><sub>b</sub> per voltage level
(transformer ratios) are chosen. Every quantity is divided by its base:</p>
<div class="eq">Z<sub>b</sub> = U<sub>b</sub>²/S<sub>b</sub> &nbsp;&nbsp; I<sub>b</sub> = S<sub>b</sub>/(√3·U<sub>b</sub>) &nbsp;&nbsp; z<sub>pu</sub> = Z/Z<sub>b</sub> &nbsp;&nbsp;
change of base: z₂ = z₁·(S<sub>b2</sub>/S<sub>b1</sub>)·(U<sub>b1</sub>/U<sub>b2</sub>)²</div>
<p>Advantages: transformers disappear (ratio 1 in pu), voltages are close to 1 pu, orders of magnitude can be compared between networks (the
<i>u</i><sub>sc</sub> of a transformer = its impedance in pu on its own base). The NRJXpert server works in pu for the load flow
(<i>S</i><sub>b</sub> = 100 MVA, <i>U</i><sub>b</sub> = network voltage entered in the calculation settings).</p>
<div class="calc" data-calc="pu"></div>

<!-- ================================================================== -->
            <a href="#top" class="back-to-top">↑ Back to top</a>
        </div>
        <div class="section" id="n2-4">
            <h2><span class="section-icon">📉</span>2.4 Voltage drop and losses</h2>
<p>For a line of impedance <i>R</i> + j<i>X</i> carrying <i>P</i> + j<i>Q</i> at line voltage <i>U</i>:</p>
<div class="eq">ΔU/U ≈ (R·P + X·Q)/U² &nbsp;&nbsp; losses: ΔP = R·(P² + Q²)/U² = 3·R·I²</div>
<p>Practical consequences: the voltage drop depends mainly on <i>P</i> in resistive networks (LV, MV with small cross-sections) and on <i>Q</i> in
inductive networks (HV); hence voltage control through reactive power in transmission, and the role of controlled inverters in distribution
(level 5). Losses vary as the square of the power and the inverse square of the voltage: doubling the voltage divides losses by 4.</p>
<div class="ex"><h4>Example 2.2 — MV feeder</h4>
<p>A 10 km, 20 kV line (<i>r</i> = 0.3 Ω/km, <i>x</i> = 0.35 Ω/km) supplies 2 MW at cos φ = 0.9 at its end. Voltage drop and losses?</p>
<details class="sol"><summary>Show the solution</summary>
<p><i>R</i> = 3 Ω, <i>X</i> = 3.5 Ω, <i>Q</i> = 2 × tan(25.84°) = 0.969 Mvar. ΔU/U = (3 × 2 + 3.5 × 0.969)/20² = <span class="result">2.35 %</span> (470 V);
losses = 3 × (2² + 0.969²)/20² = 0.037 MW = <span class="result">37 kW (1.9 %)</span>.</p>
</details></div>
<h3>Reactive power compensation</h3>
<p>A shunt compensator placed near the loads supplies locally the reactive power they consume: the reactive power flowing in the lines
decreases, and with it the voltage drop (<i>X·Q</i> term) and the losses (<i>R·Q²</i> term). A capacitor bank with <i>n</i> steps switched on
supplies <i>Q</i> = <i>n</i>·<i>Q</i><sub>step</sub>·<i>V</i>² (constant impedance: it supplies less when the voltage falls). Shunt reactors
absorb reactive power: they lower the voltage of lightly loaded or highly capacitive networks (long cables). With automatic control, a step
is switched on when the voltage leaves the dead band from below and switched off when it leaves it from above; the band must be wider than
the voltage change caused by one step, otherwise the steps hunt (palette: <b>Power system (load flow) › Switched shunt compensator</b>).</p>
<div class="ex"><h4>Example 2.9 — Compensated IEEE 33-bus network (<code>exemples/compensation_ieee33.xml</code>)</h4>
<p>Two switched compensators are placed at buses 30 (6 × 0.2 Mvar) and 14 (4 × 0.15 Mvar), with automatic control (setpoint 0.95 pu,
dead band 2 %). Open the example with <b>Help › Open a tutorial example</b>, run the load flow (Newton-Raphson, <kbd>F5</kbd>), read the
steps switched on in the compensators' tooltips, then repeat the calculation without compensation (control « None », 0 step switched on).</p>
<details class="sol"><summary>Show the results</summary>
<table>
<tr><th></th><th>Without compensation</th><th>With compensation</th></tr>
<tr><td>Steps switched on</td><td class="num">—</td><td class="num">4/6 (bus 30), 3/4 (bus 14)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Reactive power supplied</td><td class="num">—</td><td class="num">0.71 + 0.40 Mvar</td></tr>
<tr><td>Minimum voltage (bus 18)</td><td class="num">0.913 pu</td><td class="num">0.936 pu</td></tr>
<tr><td>Losses</td><td class="num">202.7 kW</td><td class="num"><span class="result">140.7 kW (−31 %)</span></td></tr>
<tr><td>Reactive power drawn at the substation</td><td class="num">2.44 Mvar</td><td class="num">1.28 Mvar</td></tr>
</table>
<p>The voltages of buses 30 and 14 (0.944 and 0.941 pu) lie within the band [0.94; 0.96] pu: one more step would push them above it, one
less below it.</p>
</details></div>
<p>A <b>STATCOM</b> (or an SVC) regulates the voltage continuously, without steps, along its characteristic <i>V</i> = <i>V</i><sub>ref</sub> −
slope·<i>Q</i>/<i>Q</i><sub>max</sub>. At its limit, the STATCOM keeps its maximum current (<i>Q</i> = <i>Q</i><sub>max</sub>·<i>V</i>) whereas
the SVC is only a susceptance (<i>Q</i> = <i>Q</i><sub>max</sub>·<i>V</i>²): it supplies less reactive power when the voltage collapses, which
is the STATCOM's advantage. In a short circuit, the STATCOM delivers its limited current (like an inverter); the SVC is neglected (palette:
<b>Power system (load flow) › Static var compensator</b>; not available in demonstration mode).</p>
<div class="ex"><h4>Example 2.10 — STATCOM at bus 30 (<code>exemples/statcom_ieee33.xml</code>)</h4>
<p>The switched compensator of bus 30 is replaced by a +1.5 / −1 Mvar STATCOM (Vref = 0.97 pu, slope 3 %). Run the load flow
(Newton-Raphson), then reduce <i>Q</i><sub>max</sub> to 0.6 Mvar.</p>
<details class="sol"><summary>Show the results</summary>
<table>
<tr><th></th><th>Qmax = 1.5 Mvar</th><th>Qmax = 0.6 Mvar</th></tr>
<tr><td>STATCOM state</td><td class="num">regulating</td><td class="num">capacitive limit</td></tr>
<tr><td>Reactive power supplied</td><td class="num">0.99 Mvar</td><td class="num">0.57 Mvar (= 0.6 × 0.941)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Voltage of bus 30</td><td class="num">0.950 pu (= 0.97 − 0.03 × 0.99/1.5)</td><td class="num">0.941 pu</td></tr>
<tr><td>Losses</td><td class="num">136.1 kW</td><td class="num">144.6 kW</td></tr>
</table>
</details></div>
<h3>Voltage control with transformer taps</h3>
<p>The substation transformer is the most common means of holding the voltage of a feeder. Its primary winding carries <b>taps</b> that change the number
of turns: the ratio becomes <i>a</i> = 1 + <i>n</i>·<i>p</i> (<i>n</i> = tap position, <i>p</i> = step, often 1.25 %). A positive position puts more turns on the primary and
<b>lowers</b> the secondary voltage; a negative one raises it. An on-load tap changer changes position under load, one step at a time, when the regulated voltage
leaves the dead band around the setpoint. In NRJXpert the ratio enters the transformer's π model (load flow, short circuit, dynamics); the dialog of the three-phase
transformer (“Tap changer (OLTC)” section) offers “Nominal ratio”, “Fixed tap” or “Automatic (voltage control)”. In automatic mode the calculation moves one position at a
time until the voltage on the chosen side is inside the band; it reports the limits and hunting (dead band narrower than the effect of one step). The clock-number phase
shift can be added on request (ignored by the three-phase distribution sweep). A tap changer alone only corrects the head-of-feeder voltage: it raises or lowers the whole
feeder by the same percentage, without reducing the voltage drop along the feeder (which compensators do).</p>
<div class="ex"><h4>Example 2.11 — Tap changer at the head of the IEEE 33-bus feeder (<code>exemples/prises_ieee33.xml</code>)</h4>
<p>The IEEE 33-bus network (no compensator) is supplied through a tapped transformer (1.25 % step, ± 8 positions) that automatically regulates the voltage of bus 2:
setpoint 1.03 pu, dead band 2 %. Open the example with <b>Help › Open a tutorial example</b>, run the load flow (Newton-Raphson, <kbd>F5</kbd>) and read the
position in the transformer results. To compare, edit the <code>tapMode</code> and <code>tapPos</code> tags of the first impedance in the XML file (“fixed” and −4 for a fixed tap, or remove the tap tags).</p>
<details class="sol"><summary>Show the results</summary>
<table>
<tr><th></th><th>No tap</th><th>Automatic</th><th>Fixed tap −4</th></tr>
<tr><td>Position</td><td class="num">—</td><td class="num">−2</td><td class="num">−4</td></tr>
<tr><td>Voltage of bus 2</td><td class="num">0.997 pu</td><td class="num">1.023 pu</td><td class="num">1.050 pu</td></tr>
<tr><td>Minimum voltage (bus 18)</td><td class="num">0.913 pu</td><td class="num">0.941 pu</td><td class="num">0.971 pu</td></tr>
<tr><td>Losses</td><td class="num">202.7 kW</td><td class="num">191.2 kW</td><td class="num">180.2 kW</td></tr>
</table>
<p>The tap changer raises the whole feeder voltage by about 2.5 % for two positions; losses fall because, at constant power, the current decreases when the voltage rises.
In automatic mode the tap stops at position −2: bus 2 is then at 1.023 pu, inside the band [1.02; 1.04] pu, and bus 18 stays just above 0.94 pu. With the fixed tap −4, bus 2
is at 1.050 pu: the feeder end is no longer under-voltage, but the head of the feeder reaches the usual 1.05 pu limit as soon as the load drops. Hence the interest of automatic
control, possibly combined with compensation (examples 2.9 and 6.4).</p>
</details></div>

