        <div class="section" id="n2-5">
            <h2><span class="section-icon">🔀</span>2.5 Load flow: formulation</h2>
<p>The load flow (power flow) calculates the steady state of a network from the generated and consumed <b>powers</b>. Since powers rather than
impedances are imposed, the equations are <b>non-linear</b>.</p>
<h3>Bus types</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Type</th><th>Data</th><th>Unknowns</th><th>In NRJXpert</th></tr>
<tr><td>Slack (swing)</td><td>|V|, θ = 0</td><td>P, Q</td><td>« Slack » generator: the source substation, which covers the losses</td></tr>
<tr><td>Generation (PV)</td><td>P, |V|</td><td>Q, θ</td><td>Voltage-controlled generator</td></tr>
<tr><td>Load (PQ)</td><td>P, Q</td><td>|V|, θ</td><td>Load bus</td></tr>
</table>
<h3>Admittance matrix and equations</h3>
<p><b>Y</b><sub>bus</sub> is built element by element: for a branch of admittance <i>y<sub>ik</sub></i> between <i>i</i> and <i>k</i>, <i>y<sub>ik</sub></i>
is added to <i>Y<sub>ii</sub></i> and <i>Y<sub>kk</sub></i> and subtracted from <i>Y<sub>ik</sub></i> and <i>Y<sub>ki</sub></i>; admittances to earth
(<i>C</i>/2 of the lines) are added to the diagonal. At node <i>i</i>:</p>
<div class="eq">S<sub>i</sub>* = P<sub>i</sub> − jQ<sub>i</sub> = V<sub>i</sub>*·Σ<sub>k</sub> Y<sub>ik</sub>V<sub>k</sub><br>
P<sub>i</sub> = Σ<sub>k</sub> |V<sub>i</sub>||V<sub>k</sub>||Y<sub>ik</sub>| cos(θ<sub>ik</sub> − δ<sub>i</sub> + δ<sub>k</sub>) &nbsp;&nbsp;
Q<sub>i</sub> = −Σ<sub>k</sub> |V<sub>i</sub>||V<sub>k</sub>||Y<sub>ik</sub>| sin(θ<sub>ik</sub> − δ<sub>i</sub> + δ<sub>k</sub>)</div>

<!-- ================================================================== -->
            <a href="#top" class="back-to-top">↑ Back to top</a>
        </div>
        <div class="section" id="n2-6">
            <h2><span class="section-icon">📘</span>2.6 Solution methods</h2>
<h3>Gauss-Seidel</h3>
<div class="eq">V<sub>i</sub><sup>(n+1)</sup> = [ (P<sub>i</sub> − jQ<sub>i</sub>)/V<sub>i</sub><sup>(n)*</sup> − Σ<sub>k≠i</sub> Y<sub>ik</sub>V<sub>k</sub> ] / Y<sub>ii</sub></div>
<p>For each node the most recent voltages are used. PV node: <i>Q<sub>i</sub></i> is first calculated with the current voltages, then
|<i>V<sub>i</sub></i>| is reset to its set-point after the update. Simple, but slow convergence (tens of iterations); an acceleration factor
(1.4 to 1.6) helps.</p>
<div class="ex"><h4>Example 2.3 — One iteration by hand</h4>
<p>Two-node network in pu: node 1 slack <i>V</i>₁ = 1∠0°; line <i>z</i> = 0.02 + j0.06; node 2 load 0.8 + j0.4 pu. Start from
<i>V</i>₂ = 1∠0° and iterate down to 10⁻⁴.</p>
<details class="sol"><summary>Show the solution</summary>
<p><i>y</i> = 1/<i>z</i> = 5 − j15; <i>Y</i>₂₂ = 5 − j15, <i>Y</i>₂₁ = −5 + j15. <i>S</i>₂ = −0.8 − j0.4 (load = negative injection).</p>
<p><i>V</i>₂<sup>(1)</sup> = [(−0.8 + j0.4)/1 + (5 − j15)·1]/(5 − j15) = 1 + (−0.8 + j0.4)/(5 − j15) = 1 − 0.04 − j0.04 = 0.96 − j0.04 →
<span class="result">0.9608∠−2.39°</span>.</p>
<p>Then: 0.9575∠−2.39°; 0.95736∠−2.395°; 0.95735∠−2.395° (difference &lt; 10⁻⁴ at the 4th iteration). The final voltage is
<span class="result">0.9573 pu ∠−2.39°</span>.</p>
</details></div>
<h3>Newton-Raphson</h3>
<p>The power equations are linearised around the current state and solved for the corrections:</p>
<div class="eq">[ ΔP ; ΔQ ] = [ ∂P/∂δ &nbsp; ∂P/∂|V| ; ∂Q/∂δ &nbsp; ∂Q/∂|V| ] · [ Δδ ; Δ|V| ] &nbsp;&nbsp; (Jacobian J, recalculated at each iteration)</div>
<p><b>Quadratic</b> convergence: 3 to 6 iterations whatever the size of the network, but each iteration costs a factorisation of the Jacobian. It is
the reference method.</p>
<h3>Fast decoupled (Stott)</h3>
<p>Assumptions: R ≪ X, small angles, |V| ≈ 1. Then ∂P/∂|V| ≈ 0 and ∂Q/∂δ ≈ 0, and the sub-matrices become constant (B' and B''): they are factorised
only once. Very fast on transmission networks, but slow or divergent when R/X is large (distribution networks): see the lab below and the
distribution load flow of level 5.</p>

