<h3>The synchronous machine in the network: E, Xd, X′d and X″d</h3>
<p>The rotor of a generator, fed with direct current (the <b>excitation</b>), creates a field rotating at synchronous speed; the stator sees an EMF <i>E</i> proportional to the excitation. In steady state the machine is represented by this source <i>E</i> behind the synchronous reactance <i>X</i><sub>d</sub>:</p>
<div class="eq"><i>E</i> = <i>V</i> + j<i>X</i><sub>d</sub>·<i>I</i> &nbsp;&nbsp; ⇒ &nbsp;&nbsp; <i>P</i> = (<i>E</i>·<i>V</i>/<i>X</i><sub>d</sub>)·sin δ , &nbsp;&nbsp; <i>Q</i> = (<i>E</i>·<i>V</i>·cos δ − <i>V</i>²)/<i>X</i><sub>d</sub></div>
<p>Active power is set by the turbine torque (internal angle δ), reactive power by the excitation: an <b>over-excited</b> machine (<i>E</i> cos δ &gt; <i>V</i>) supplies reactive power and raises the voltage, an <b>under-excited</b> one absorbs it. This is why a generator is a PV bus in the load flow: its voltage regulator (AVR) adjusts <i>E</i> to hold <i>V</i>, and <i>Q</i> is a result of the calculation.</p>
<p>During a short circuit the rotor flux cannot change instantly: the apparent reactance of the machine goes from <b>X″<sub>d</sub></b> (subtransient, first 10 to 50 ms), to <b>X′<sub>d</sub></b> (transient, up to a few seconds) and then <b>X<sub>d</sub></b> (steady state). The lower the reactance, the larger the current: from 5–8 times rated current at the start to 1–2 times in steady state. Each study therefore uses the reactance that matches its time scale:</p>
<table><tr><th>Study</th><th>Reactance</th><th>Typical values (pu)</th></tr>
<tr><td>Load flow (steady state)</td><td>X<sub>d</sub> (for the excitation limit)</td><td>1.2 to 2.2</td></tr>
<tr><td>Dynamics (stability, PSS)</td><td>X′<sub>d</sub> and T′<sub>d0</sub> (flux time constant)</td><td>0.2 to 0.4; 4 to 8 s</td></tr>
<tr><td>Short circuit IEC 60909 (I″<sub>k</sub>)</td><td>X″<sub>d</sub> (corrected by the factor K<sub>G</sub>)</td><td>0.12 to 0.25</td></tr></table>
<p>A <b>synchronous motor</b> is the same machine with negative active power: it consumes <i>P</i> but can still supply or absorb reactive power according to its excitation. Its short-circuit contribution is that of a generator.</p>
<h3>Reactive power limits of generators</h3>
<p>A PV node is held only if the generator can supply the necessary reactive power. The P-Q diagram (capability curve) of an alternator is
bounded by the stator current (<i>p</i>² + <i>q</i>² ≤ <i>v</i>²), the field current (<i>p</i>² + (<i>q</i> + <i>v</i>²/<i>X</i><sub>d</sub>)² ≤
(<i>v</i>·<i>E</i><sub>fd max</sub>/<i>X</i><sub>d</sub>)², rotor heating), the under-excitation limit (stability, absorbed reactive power) and the
turbine power. When the requested reactive power exceeds a limit, the load flow sets <i>Q</i> at that limit: the node becomes PQ and its voltage is
no longer held; it becomes PV again if the voltage comes back to the right side of the set-point (<b>Capability, AVR, PSS…</b> button of the
generator dialog).</p>
