            <h2><span class="section-icon">⚡</span>2.7 Short circuit according to IEC 60909-0</h2>
<h3>Principle: equivalent voltage source</h3>
<p>All sources are replaced by a single source <i>c·U</i><sub>n</sub>/√3 at the fault point; loads are neglected (except motors) and the network is
reduced to the impedance seen from the fault. The voltage factor <i>c</i> covers voltage variations and approximations:</p>
<table>
<tr><th>Nominal voltage</th><th>c<sub>max</sub> (breaking capacity, withstand)</th><th>c<sub>min</sub> (protection settings)</th></tr>
<tr><td>U<sub>n</sub> ≤ 1 kV, tolerance +6 %</td><td class="num">1.05</td><td class="num">0.95</td></tr>
<tr><td>U<sub>n</sub> ≤ 1 kV, tolerance +10 %</td><td class="num">1.10</td><td class="num">0.95</td></tr>
<tr><td>U<sub>n</sub> &gt; 1 kV</td><td class="num">1.10</td><td class="num">1.00</td></tr>
</table>
<h3>Symmetrical components</h3>
<p>An unbalanced three-phase system is decomposed (Fortescue) into three balanced systems: positive (1), negative (2) and zero (0) sequence, with
<i>a</i> = e<sup>j2π/3</sup>:</p>
<div class="eq">[V<sub>a</sub> ; V<sub>b</sub> ; V<sub>c</sub>] = [1 1 1 ; 1 a² a ; 1 a a²]·[V₀ ; V₁ ; V₂]</div>
<p>Each element has three impedances <i>Z</i>₁, <i>Z</i>₂, <i>Z</i>₀ (static elements: <i>Z</i>₂ = <i>Z</i>₁); the impedance seen from the fault is
calculated in each of the three networks, which are then combined according to the type of fault:</p>
<table>
<tr><th>Fault</th><th>Initial symmetrical current I"k</th><th>Combination of the networks</th></tr>
<tr><td>Three-phase</td><td>c·U<sub>n</sub> / (√3·|Z₁|)</td><td>positive sequence only</td></tr>
<tr><td>Phase-to-phase</td><td>c·U<sub>n</sub> / |Z₁ + Z₂|</td><td>positive and negative in opposition</td></tr>
<tr><td>Two-phase-to-earth</td><td>earth current I"<sub>kE2E</sub> = √3·c·U<sub>n</sub>·|Z₂| / |Z₁Z₂ + Z₁Z₀ + Z₂Z₀|</td><td>negative ∥ zero</td></tr>
<tr><td>Phase-to-earth</td><td>√3·c·U<sub>n</sub> / |Z₁ + Z₂ + Z₀|</td><td>all three in series</td></tr>
</table>
<p>Peak current: <i>i</i><sub>p</sub> = κ·√2·I"<sub>k</sub> with κ = 1.02 + 0.98·e<sup>−3R/X</sup>. Fault far from generators: breaking current
<i>I</i><sub>b</sub> and steady-state current <i>I</i><sub>k</sub> equal to I"<sub>k</sub>. Short-circuit power: S"<sub>k</sub> = √3·U<sub>n</sub>·I"<sub>k</sub>.</p>
<div class="calc" data-calc="icc3"></div>

<div class="ex"><h4>Example 2.4 — Faults at the end of a 20 kV feeder</h4>
