<h3>Parallel lines: zero-sequence mutual coupling</h3>
<p>Two circuits on the same tower (or two cables in the same trench) are magnetically coupled. In the positive and negative sequences the
three phases compensate each other and the coupling is negligible; in the zero sequence the three currents are in phase and return through
the earth: the mutual impedance <i>Z</i><sub>0m</sub> reaches 40 to 70 % of <i>Z</i>₀. For two parallel lines between the same nodes, with
zero-sequence impedances <i>Z</i><sub>A</sub> and <i>Z</i><sub>B</sub>:</p>
<div class="eq">Z<sub>0,eq</sub> = (Z<sub>A</sub>·Z<sub>B</sub> − Z<sub>0m</sub>²) / (Z<sub>A</sub> + Z<sub>B</sub> − 2·Z<sub>0m</sub>) &nbsp;&nbsp; = (Z<sub>0</sub> + Z<sub>0m</sub>)/2 for two identical lines</div>
<p>The coupling therefore increases <i>Z</i>₀ seen from an earth fault and reduces its current; it also induces a zero-sequence current in the
sound line, which distorts the measurement of distance protections (earth compensation factor). In NRJXpert: « Zero-sequence coupling with a
parallel line » section of the line (coupled line, <i>r</i><sub>0m</sub>, <i>x</i><sub>0m</sub> in Ω/km, direction), taken into account in the
zero-sequence network of the short-circuit calculation.</p>
