The three-phase fault (3 115 A) is unchanged: the coupling acts only in the zero sequence.</p>
</details></div>
<h3>Induction machine: equivalent circuit, slip and torque</h3>
<p>The rotor of an induction machine is not supplied: the rotating stator field (speed <i>n</i><sub>s</sub> = 60·<i>f</i>/<i>p</i>) induces currents in it as soon as the rotor turns at a different speed <i>n</i>. The <b>slip</b> is defined as <i>s</i> = (<i>n</i><sub>s</sub> − <i>n</i>)/<i>n</i><sub>s</sub>: 1 at standstill, 2 to 4 % at rated load, negative for a generator (squirrel-cage wind turbine). Per phase, the machine is represented by <i>R</i><sub>s</sub> + j<i>X</i><sub>s</sub> in series with the parallel combination of j<i>X</i><sub>m</sub> (magnetisation) and <i>R</i><sub>r</sub>/<i>s</i> + j<i>X</i><sub>r</sub> (rotor). The resistance <i>R</i><sub>r</sub>/<i>s</i> = <i>R</i><sub>r</sub> + <i>R</i><sub>r</sub>(1 − <i>s</i>)/<i>s</i> contains the mechanical power: <i>P</i><sub>mech</sub> = 3·<i>I</i>r²·<i>R</i><sub>r</sub>·(1 − <i>s</i>)/<i>s</i>.</p>
<p>Two fundamental consequences for the network engineer:</p>
<ul>
<li><b>At start (<i>s</i> = 1)</b>, <i>R</i><sub>r</sub>/<i>s</i> is small: the machine behaves as a low impedance and draws <b>5 to 7 times its rated current</b> at a power factor of 0.2 to 0.3. This current causes the starting voltage dip.</li>
<li><b>Torque varies as the square of the voltage</b>: neglecting the magnetising branch, <i>T</i> ≈ (3/ω<sub>s</sub>)·<i>V</i>²·(<i>R</i><sub>r</sub>/<i>s</i>)/[(<i>R</i><sub>s</sub> + <i>R</i><sub>r</sub>/<i>s</i>)² + (<i>X</i><sub>s</sub> + <i>X</i><sub>r</sub>)²]. At 80 % voltage the torque falls to 64 %. The slip at maximum torque does not depend on voltage; if the load torque exceeds the available maximum torque, the motor <b>stalls</b>: it slows down, its current stays high, which lowers the voltage even further.</li>
</ul>
<p>Finally, an induction machine always consumes reactive power (magnetisation and leakage: 0.3 to 0.5 times its rated active power): in the load flow it is a PQ load whose slip (hence <i>P</i> and <i>Q</i>) NRJXpert computes for the bus voltage.</p>
