        <div class="section" id="n2-8">
            <h2><span class="section-icon">⚡</span>2.8 Protection: what short-circuit currents are used for</h2>
<ul>
<li><b>Breaking capacity</b> of the device ≥ maximum I"k (and ip ≤ making capacity) at the point of installation.</li>
<li><b>Sensitivity</b>: the <b>minimum</b> short-circuit current (c<sub>min</sub>, furthest fault, often phase-to-earth or phase-to-phase) must trip the
protection within the required time: this is the « minimum Isc » calculation of NRJXpert.</li>
<li><b>Thermal withstand of cables</b>: I²·t ≤ k²·S² (k ≈ 143 for XLPE-insulated copper).</li>
<li><b>Selectivity</b>: only the protection immediately upstream of the fault must trip (current, time or logic selectivity).</li>
<li><b>Inverters</b>: their contribution is limited to 1.1 to 1.5 times their rated current; a network supplied mainly by inverters has low fault
currents, which makes detection harder (level 5).</li>
</ul>
<h3>The protection chain: measure, decide, interrupt</h3>
<p>A protection is always a chain of three functions. The <b>sensors</b> (current transformers CT and voltage transformers VT) scale network quantities down to values that can be measured safely (1 or 5 A, 100 V). The <b>relay</b> compares these measurements with its settings and decides. The <b>interrupting device</b> carries out the order.</p>
<div class="fig" id="fig-chaine-prot"><svg viewBox="0 0 560 150" width="560" role="img" aria-label="Protection chain: CT and VT, relay, breaker" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" font-family="inherit"><path d="M20 100 L540 100"/><circle cx="110" cy="100" r="9"/><circle cx="122" cy="100" r="9"/><text x="116" y="124" text-anchor="middle" font-size="12" fill="currentColor" stroke="none">TC</text><path d="M200 100 L200 118"/><rect x="186" y="118" width="28" height="14" rx="2"/><text x="200" y="146" text-anchor="middle" font-size="12" fill="currentColor" stroke="none">TT</text><rect x="400" y="88" width="30" height="24" rx="2"/><text x="415" y="130" text-anchor="middle" font-size="12" fill="currentColor" stroke="none">Breaker</text><rect x="240" y="20" width="130" height="40" rx="6"/><text x="305" y="45" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13" fill="currentColor" stroke="none">Relay 50/51, 21, 87</text><path d="M116 91 L116 40 L240 40"/><path d="M200 118 L200 110"/><path d="M200 100 L200 70 L240 52"/><path d="M370 40 L415 40 L415 88"/><text x="150" y="34" font-size="11.5" fill="currentColor" stroke="none">I</text><text x="206" y="82" font-size="11.5" fill="currentColor" stroke="none">U</text><text x="420" y="66" font-size="11.5" fill="currentColor" stroke="none">trip order</text><text x="40" y="92" font-size="11.5" fill="currentColor" stroke="none">source</text><text x="480" y="92" font-size="11.5" fill="currentColor" stroke="none">line</text></svg><div class="cap">Protection chain: instrument transformers (CT, VT) scale current and voltage down to values the relay can read; the relay decides; the breaker interrupts. A fuse combines the three functions in a single device.</div></div>
<ul>
<li>The <b>circuit breaker</b> interrupts any current up to its breaking capacity and can be closed again; the relay gives it the order.</li>
<li>The <b>fuse</b> combines sensor, relay and interruption: the element melts when the <i>I</i>²·<i>t</i> energy exceeds its pre-arcing value. At short-circuit currents it clears in less than half a cycle, before the peak (it “limits” the current), but must be replaced after each operation. Practical rule: two gG fuses in series are selective if the upstream rating is at least 1.6 times the downstream rating.</li>
<li>The <b>recloser</b> is a circuit breaker with a relay that closes again automatically: on an overhead line, 70 to 80 % of faults are transient (arc in air, tree branch) and disappear after a few hundred ms without voltage.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Why inverse-time curves?</h4>
<p>The heating of a cable or transformer is proportional to <i>I</i>²·<i>t</i>: doubling the current divides the permissible time by four. An inverse-time protection follows this law: it tolerates a moderate overload for a long time (motor start, energisation) but trips quickly on a short circuit. The three families of IEC 60255-151 (SI, VI, EI) differ in steepness. The threshold <i>I</i><sub>s</sub> fixes the asymptote, the TMS multiplier shifts the curve vertically. A fault current flows through every device in series between the source and the fault; for only the device closest to the fault to trip, its curve must lie <b>below</b> that of the upstream device, with at least 0.3 s between them. Two curves that cross are not selective.</p>
<h4>Distance protection (21)</h4>
<p>The relay divides voltage by current: <i>Z</i> = <i>V</i>/<i>I</i>. Since the impedance of a line is proportional to its length, <i>Z</i> measures the <b>distance to the fault</b> independently of current magnitude, which suits networks whose short-circuit power varies. Three zones (circular “mho” characteristics through the origin) are set on the line impedance: zone 1 at 80 % (instantaneous: 100 % is not used because of measurement and data errors, otherwise it would trip for a fault on the next line), zone 2 at 120 % with 0.3 s (covers the end of the line and acts as back-up), zone 3 at 200 % with 1 s. For an earth fault the loop impedance involves <i>Z</i><sub>0</sub>; the relay corrects it with the factor <i>k</i><sub>0</sub> = (<i>Z</i><sub>0</sub> − <i>Z</i><sub>1</sub>)/(3<i>Z</i><sub>1</sub>), which the mutual coupling of a parallel line distorts (the relay then underestimates the distance: “under-reach”).</p>
<h4>Differential protection (87)</h4>
<p>It applies Kirchhoff's current law to the protected element (line, transformer, busbar): with no internal fault the sum of the incoming currents is zero; if it is not (allowing for a restraint proportional to through current, to cover CT errors) the fault is <b>inside</b> the zone and tripping is immediate. Selectivity is absolute, with no time delay: in return, a line needs a communication link between its two ends, and a transformer needs inrush restraint (not modelled in NRJXpert).</p>
<h4>Common mistakes</h4>
<ul>
<li>Setting the threshold <i>I</i><sub>s</sub> below the maximum load current: nuisance trips. It must sit above it (margin of 1.2 to 1.5) and well below the <b>minimum</b> short-circuit current (furthest fault, phase-to-phase or single-phase): otherwise the protection does not see the fault.</li>
<li>Checking selectivity with the maximum <i>I</i>″<sub>k</sub> only: trip time changes with current, so the curves must be examined at both extremes.</li>
<li>Forgetting CT saturation: beyond its accuracy limit factor it distorts the current and the relay trips too late (see below).</li>
<li>Relying on an ordinary earth-fault protection in an isolated or compensated network: the residual current is only a few amperes, a wattmetric or directional earth-fault protection is needed.</li>
<li>Confusing breaking capacity (the maximum current the device can interrupt) with the trip threshold (the current above which the relay orders the interruption).</li>
</ul>
<h3>Protection plan and selectivity in NRJXpert</h3>
<p>A switching device of breaker or recloser type receives a protection (<b>Protection</b> section of its dialog); a fuse has its rating. Available
protections: <b>overcurrent</b> 50/51 (IEC 60255-151 inverse curves: <i>t</i> = TMS·<i>k</i>/((<i>I</i>/<i>I</i><sub>s</sub>)<sup>α</sup> − 1),
SI: <i>k</i> = 0.14, α = 0.02; VI: 13.5 and 1; EI: 80 and 2; IEEE and definite-time curves), with high-set I&gt;&gt; and earth protection
50N/51N on 3·<i>I</i><sub>0</sub>; LV breaker <b>electronic trip unit</b> (long time <i>I</i><sup>2</sup><i>t</i>, short time, instantaneous);
<b>distance</b> 21 (apparent impedance <i>V</i>/<i>I</i>, three mho zones, typically 80 % instantaneous, 120 % at 0.3 s, 200 % at 1 s);
<b>line differential</b> 87L. In the short-circuit calculation the server gives the operating time of each device and the device that clears the
fault; two devices are deemed selective if their times differ by at least 0.3 s (breaker opening time, relay overshoot and safety margin). The
<b>Selectivity…</b> button plots the time-current curves.</p>
