<div class="ex"><h4>Example 2.15 — Time selectivity of two feeders (<code>exemples/selectivite_depart.xml</code>)</h4>
<p>The network of example 2.4 supplies a line LA (3 + j3.5 Ω) protected by breaker S1, then a line LB (2 + j2 Ω) protected by S2. SI relays:
S1 <i>I</i><sub>s</sub> = 400 A, TMS = 0.3; S2 <i>I</i><sub>s</sub> = 200 A, TMS = 0.1; breaker opening 50 ms. Three-phase fault at the end of
LB. Compute the times, then find the TMS of S1 that keeps a 0.3 s margin as fast as possible.</p>
<details class="sol"><summary>Show the solution</summary>
<p>I"k = 1 400 A, seen by S1 and S2. S2: <i>I</i>/<i>I</i><sub>s</sub> = 7.0, <i>t</i> = 0.1 × 0.14/(7.0<sup>0.02</sup> − 1) + 0.05 =
<span class="result">0.403 s</span>: S2 clears the fault. S1: <i>I</i>/<i>I</i><sub>s</sub> = 3.5, <i>t</i> = 0.3 × 0.14/(3.5<sup>0.02</sup> − 1)
+ 0.05 = 1.705 s: selective, but slow for a fault on LA (1.33 s for a fault at the end of LA, I"k = 2 012 A). With TMS = 0.12:
<span class="result">0.712 s</span>, i.e. 0.31 s after S2; below that the calculation reports insufficient selectivity. The phase-to-earth fault
(949 A) gives 0.49 s and 2.46 s: the earth protection (lower pickup on 3·<i>I</i><sub>0</sub>) speeds up the clearing of earth faults.</p>
</details></div>
<h3>Instrument transformers and surge arresters</h3>
<p>A relay sees the network only through its <b>current</b> (CT) and <b>voltage transformers</b> (VT). A 5P20 protection CT keeps a composite
error below 5 % up to 20 times its rated current, under its rated burden; with a different actual burden <i>S</i><sub>b</sub>, the actual
accuracy limit factor is ALF′ = ALF·(<i>R</i><sub>ct</sub>·<i>I</i><sub>sn</sub>² + <i>S</i><sub>n</sub>)/(<i>R</i><sub>ct</sub>·<i>I</i><sub>sn</sub>² +
<i>S</i><sub>b</sub>). Beyond it the CT saturates: the relay sees less current and trips later. A metal-oxide <b>surge arrester</b> limits
lightning and switching overvoltages: the voltage at the equipment is <i>U</i><sub>eq</sub> = <i>U</i><sub>pl</sub> + 2·<i>S</i>·<i>L</i>/<i>v</i>
(steepness <i>S</i>, distance <i>L</i>, <i>v</i> = 300 m/µs) and must stay at least 20 % below the lightning impulse withstand (BIL) of the
equipment (IEC 60071-2). It must also withstand the <b>temporary overvoltages</b> of earth faults: with isolated or compensated neutral, the
sound phases rise to √3 times the phase voltage, hence a higher rated voltage <i>U</i><sub>r</sub> than with an earthed neutral.</p>
<div class="ex"><h4>Example 2.16 — Arrester in an isolated-neutral network (<code>exemples/parafoudre_neutre_isole.xml</code>)</h4>
<p>20 kV network (<i>U</i><sub>m</sub> = 24 kV) with isolated neutral, zero-sequence capacitance 1 µF; arrester PF1 (<i>U</i><sub>c</sub> = 15.3 kV,
<i>U</i><sub>r</sub> = 21 kV, <i>U</i><sub>pl</sub> = 70 kV, T = 1.0) 5 m from a transformer with a 125 kV BIL, 1 000 kV/µs wave. Phase-to-earth
fault at the customer. Check the coordination, then repeat with an earthed neutral.</p>
<details class="sol"><summary>Show the solution</summary>
<p>Lightning: <i>U</i><sub>eq</sub> = 70 + 2 × 1 000 × 5/300 = 103.3 kV, margin 125/103.3 = <span class="result">1.21</span> ≥ 1.2: satisfied
(at 6 m it falls below 1.2). Isolated neutral: capacitive fault current of only 12 A, but <span class="result">k = 1.74</span> at the arrester:
temporary overvoltage 1.74 × 24/√3 = 24.1 kV &gt; T·<i>U</i><sub>r</sub> = 21 kV; the calculation asks for <i>U</i><sub>r</sub> ≥ 24.1 kV (a 24 kV
arrester with T = 1.05 is suitable). Earthed neutral: fault current 1 169 A, k = 1.18, overvoltage 16.4 kV: the 21 kV arrester is suitable.</p>
</details></div>
<h3>Neutral earthing system: isolated, resistance, compensated or solid</h3>
<p>The current of an earth fault depends mainly on how the network neutral is earthed. With an <b>isolated neutral</b>, it returns only through the
zero-sequence capacitances of lines and cables: <i>I</i><sub>c</sub> = 3·ω·<i>C</i><sub>0</sub>·<i>U</i><sub>n</sub>/√3 (a few amperes to a few hundred
amperes); operation can continue, but the sound phases rise to √3·<i>V</i>. A <b>neutral resistor</b> limits the current (typically 300 to 1 000 A in
MV) while allowing a simple earth protection. The <b>Petersen coil</b> compensates the capacitive current: tuned (ω·<i>L</i> = 1/(3·ω·<i>C</i><sub>0</sub>)),
it leaves only a small residual current ≈ <i>I</i><sub>c</sub>·√(<i>v</i>² + <i>r</i>²) (detuning <i>v</i>, active current <i>r</i> of the parallel resistor),
which extinguishes most transient faults; detection then requires wattmetric protections. With a <b>solidly earthed neutral</b>, the current is
high and the overvoltages low (<i>k</i> ≤ 1.4).</p>
<div class="fig" id="fig-neutre"><svg viewBox="0 0 560 250" width="560" role="img" aria-label="Earth-fault current and Petersen coil" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" font-family="inherit"><path d="M20 190H540"/><path d="M110 40H480"/><text x="492" y="44" text-anchor="start" font-size="12" fill="currentColor" stroke="none">a</text><path d="M110 80H480"/><text x="492" y="84" text-anchor="start" font-size="12" fill="currentColor" stroke="none">b</text><path d="M110 120H480"/><text x="492" y="124" text-anchor="start" font-size="12" fill="currentColor" stroke="none">c</text><path d="M60 80L110 40M60 80H110M60 80L110 120"/><circle cx="60" cy="80" r="3" fill="currentColor"/><path d="M60 80V104"/><rect x="48" y="104" width="24" height="34" rx="5"/><text x="60" y="124.5" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10" fill="currentColor" stroke="none"></text><path d="M60 138V190"/><text x="40" y="126" text-anchor="end" font-size="12" fill="currentColor" stroke="none">Zn</text><path d="M300 40V140 M291 140H309 M291 146H309 M300 146V190"/><path d="M300 80V140 M291 140H309 M291 146H309 M300 146V190"/><path d="M300 120V140 M291 140H309 M291 146H309 M300 146V190"/><text x="340" y="142" text-anchor="start" font-size="11" fill="currentColor" stroke="none">C0: capacitance of each phase</text><path d="M440 40L432 62L448 76L440 100"/><path d="M440 100L440 118"/><path d="M437.274 111.553L440 118L442.726 111.553" fill="currentColor"/><path d="M440 118V190"/><text x="428" y="104" text-anchor="end" font-size="11.5" fill="currentColor" stroke="none">fault</text><path d="M190 30L235 30"/><path d="M228.553 32.7259L235 30L228.553 27.2741" fill="currentColor"/><text x="212" y="22" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11.5" fill="currentColor" stroke="none">If</text><path d="M318 160L318 178" stroke-dasharray="5 4"/><path d="M315.274 171.553L318 178L320.726 171.553" fill="currentColor"/><text x="352" y="172" text-anchor="start" font-size="11" fill="currentColor" stroke="none">Ic = 3·ω·C0·V</text><path d="M86 160L86 142" stroke-dasharray="5 4"/><path d="M88.7259 148.447L86 142L83.2741 148.447" fill="currentColor"/><text x="118" y="160" text-anchor="start" font-size="11" fill="currentColor" stroke="none">IL = V/(ω·L')</text><text x="280" y="218" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11" fill="currentColor" stroke="none">Zn = ∞: isolated; Zn = R: resistor; Zn = jω·L: Petersen coil (tuned: 3·ω²·L·C0 = 1) ; Zn = 0: solidly earthed</text><text x="280" y="234" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11" fill="currentColor" stroke="none"></text></svg><div class="cap">The single-phase fault can only close through the capacitances C0 of the other two phases (current Ic, a few amperes to a few hundred amperes) and through the neutral impedance Zn. With an isolated neutral there is only Ic; with a Petersen coil IL opposes Ic: the two currents subtract and only a residual current of a few amperes remains, which extinguishes most transient faults. In every system except solid earthing the healthy phases rise to √3 times the phase voltage.</div></div>
<div class="ex"><h4>Example 2.17 — The four neutral earthing systems of a 20 kV network (<code>exemples/reseau_compense.xml</code>)</h4>
<p>20 kV substation with isolated neutral (alternator), lines 1 + j1.5 Ω and 0.5 + j0.5 Ω, cables with a total zero-sequence capacitance of 10 µF,
24 kV arrester; earthing device « Bobine » at the substation: Petersen coil with automatic tuning, detuning +5 %, active current 3 %. Phase-to-earth
fault at the customer. Compare with the isolated neutral, a resistor limiting to 300 A and the solidly earthed neutral.</p>
<details class="sol"><summary>Show the solution</summary>
<p><i>I</i><sub>c</sub> = 3 × 314 × 10 µF × 20 000/√3 = <span class="result">108.8 A</span>; tuned coil: <i>L</i> = 1/(3 × 314² × 10 µF × 1.05) = 0.322 H.</p>
<table>
<tr><th>Earthing</th><th>Fault current</th><th>Neutral current</th><th>k at the arrester</th></tr>
<tr><td>Isolated neutral</td><td class="num">124 A (capacitive)</td><td class="num">—</td><td class="num">1.78</td></tr>
<tr><td>Petersen coil</td><td class="num"><span class="result">5.8 A</span></td><td class="num">123 A (coil)</td><td class="num">1.73</td></tr>
