            <h3>Limits of the NRJXpert models</h3>
<p>A model simplifies: you must know what it does not represent so as not to make it say what it cannot say.</p>
<ul>
<li><b>Mutual coupling</b>: zero sequence only; taken into account in the short circuit, not in the distribution sweep.</li>
<li><b>Induction machine</b>: a single rotor cage; the doubly-fed machine (DFIG) is not modelled separately (a converter-connected wind turbine is represented by an inverter-based generator).</li>
<li><b>Generator</b>: the capability curve acts in the classic load flow; the PSS tuning must be checked for each configuration.</li>
<li><b>Protection</b>: it does not trip in dynamic simulations; no transformer differential nor wattmetric or directional earth-fault protection (67N); time grading only; CT saturation by simple clipping.</li>
<li><b>Surge arresters</b>: coordination by a static formula (<i>U</i><sub>pl</sub> + 2·<i>S</i>·<i>L</i>/<i>v</i>); lightning and switching overvoltages are not simulated.</li>
<li><b>Neutral earthing</b>: steady state (no arc transient); the zero-sequence capacitance <i>C</i><sub>0</sub> is that of the whole network, which is wrong if transformers separate several zones.</li>
</ul>
