            <h2><span class="section-icon">🎯</span>Exercises</h2>
<div class="ex"><h4>Exercise 1 — Short circuit at the secondary of a transformer</h4>
<p>The transformer of example 2.1 is supplied by a network of infinite short-circuit power. Calculate the three-phase I"k at the secondary
(c = 1.05), κ and ip.</p>
<details class="sol"><summary>Show the solution</summary>
<p>I"k = 1.05 × 400/(√3 × 0.0256) = <span class="result">9.47 kA</span>; R/X = 0.344; κ = 1.02 + 0.98·e<sup>−1.031</sup> = 1.370;
ip = 1.370 × √2 × 9.47 = <span class="result">18.3 kA</span>. The LV main breaker must have a breaking capacity of at least 10 kA (15 or 25 kA is
chosen to account for the actual short-circuit power of the upstream network).</p>
</details></div>
<div class="ex"><h4>Exercise 2 — Change of base</h4>
<p>A 2 MVA, 30/0.4 kV transformer has <i>u</i><sub>sc</sub> = 6 %. Express its impedance in pu on a 100 MVA base, then in ohms on the 30 kV side.</p>
<details class="sol"><summary>Show the solution</summary>
<p><i>z</i> = 0.06 × 100/2 = <span class="result">3 pu</span>; <i>Z</i><sub>b</sub> = 30²/100 = 9 Ω; <i>Z</i> = <span class="result">27 Ω</span>
(= 0.06 × 30²/2).</p>
</details></div>
<div class="ex"><h4>Exercise 3 — Voltage profile</h4>
<p>In lab 3, the load becomes 8 MW, 4 Mvar. Without detailed calculation, what will happen to V3, to Qgen of G2 and to the losses? Check with
NRJXpert.</p>
<details class="sol"><summary>Show the solution</summary>
<p>Forecast: V3 decreases (≈ (R·P + X·Q)/U² increases); G2, which holds its voltage, supplies more reactive power; losses grow roughly as the
square of the flows. Calculation (Newton-Raphson, 3 iterations): V3 = <span class="result">30.629 kV</span> (−1.23°) instead of 30.937 kV; Qgen of
G2 = <span class="result">3.71 Mvar</span> instead of 1.16; the substation supplies 5.17 MW and 0.53 Mvar; losses = 5.172 + 3 − 8 =
<span class="result">172 kW</span>, i.e. 2.9 times more for a load 1.6 times larger.</p>
</details></div>
<div class="ex"><h4>Exercise 4 — Iterative methods</h4>
<p>Explain why Newton-Raphson converges in 3 iterations from any reasonable initialisation (« flat » voltages 1∠0°), while Gauss-Seidel needs 20.
What happens if the load becomes too large (beyond the maximum transmissible power)?</p>
<details class="sol"><summary>Show the solution</summary>
<p>NR uses the derivatives (tangent): near the solution the error is squared at each iteration. GS corrects each node separately, with linear
convergence. Beyond the voltage collapse point (nose of the P-V curve) there is no solution any more: no method converges and the server returns
a non-convergence message.</p>
</details></div>
<div class="ex"><h4>Exercise 5 — Compensating an MV feeder</h4>
<p>A 20 kV source supplies, through a feeder of impedance 2 + j6 Ω, a 5 MW and 3 Mvar load. Draw the network (20 kV slack bus,
impedance, load bus) and answer:</p>
<ol>
<li>Voltage drop and losses with the approximate formulas, then with the load flow (Newton-Raphson).</li>
<li>Reactive power to supply at the load bus to limit the voltage drop to 3 % (approximate formula).</li>
<li>Place a 4 × 1 Mvar switched shunt compensator (palette <b>Power system (load flow)</b>): how many steps should be switched on?
Check with the load flow; why is the reactive power supplied lower than <i>n</i> × 1 Mvar?</li>
<li>Set the compensator to automatic control (setpoint 0.98 pu, dead band 2 %): what does the calculation choose?</li>
<li>Replace the compensator by a ± 4 Mvar STATCOM holding the voltage at 1.00 pu (zero slope). What happens? What rating is needed, and
is it desirable as far as losses are concerned?</li>
</ol>
<details class="sol"><summary>Show the solution</summary>
<p>1. ΔU/U ≈ (R·P + X·Q)/U² = (2 × 5 + 6 × 3)/20² = 7 %, losses ≈ R·(P² + Q²)/U² = 2 × 34/400 = 0.17 MW. Load flow:
<span class="result">0.922 pu</span> (7.8 % drop) and <span class="result">0.200 MW</span>: the approximate formulas assume the rated voltage
at the load bus, whereas the current there is higher because the voltage is lower.</p>
<p>2. (10 + 6·(3 − <i>Q</i><sub>c</sub>))/400 ≤ 0.03 ⇒ <i>Q</i><sub>c</sub> ≥ <span class="result">2.67 Mvar</span>.</p>
<p>3. Three steps: <span class="result">0.968 pu</span>, losses 0.134 MW (−33 %), reactive power supplied only 2.81 Mvar, since a capacitor
supplies <i>Q</i> = <i>n</i>·<i>Q</i><sub>step</sub>·<i>V</i>² (3 × 0.968² = 2.81). Two steps are not enough (0.952 pu).</p>
<p>4. The band is [0.97; 0.99] pu: the calculation switches on <span class="result">4 steps</span> (0.985 pu), three giving 0.968 pu,
below the band.</p>
<p>5. The STATCOM reaches its <b>capacitive limit</b>: 3.94 Mvar (= 4 × 0.986), voltage 0.986 pu, warning from the calculation. To hold
1.00 pu it needs <span class="result">4.9 Mvar</span>, and the losses rise again (0.143 MW against 0.133 MW): beyond the reactive power
consumed by the load, the feeder carries reactive power back to the substation. A slope of a few % avoids this over-compensation.</p>
</details></div>
<div class="ex"><h4>Exercise 6 — Tap changer at the head of the feeder</h4>
<p>Take the feeder of exercise 5 (20 kV source, impedance 2 + j6 Ω, load 5 MW and 3 Mvar, voltage 0.922 pu at the load bus). A tapped 20 kV / 20 kV transformer
(1.25 % step, ± 8 positions; short-circuit impedance neglected) is placed between the source and the feeder.</p>
<ol>
<li>Estimate the position needed to bring the load voltage back to at least 0.98 pu. Check with the load flow with a fixed tap, for positions −2, −3, −4 and −5.</li>
<li>Switch to automatic control (regulated voltage: secondary side of the transformer, setpoint 1.00 pu, dead band 2 %). What does the calculation choose?</li>
<li>Reduce the dead band to 0.5 %. What does the calculation report, and why?</li>
</ol>
<details class="sol"><summary>Show the solution</summary>
<p>1. Each position raises the voltage by about 1.25 % (slightly more at the load bus, because the current decreases): from 0.922 to 0.98 pu takes a little more than 4 positions.
Load flow: <span class="result">0.950; 0.965; 0.979; 0.995 pu</span> for −2; −3; −4; −5. Position −4 is just below 0.98 pu: <span class="result">−5</span> is needed.</p>
<p>2. The band is [0.99; 1.01] pu: the calculation stops at <span class="result">position −5</span> (0.995 pu); −4 gives 0.979 pu, below the band.</p>
<p>3. A <b>hunting</b> warning: one position changes the voltage by 1.5 %, more than the width of the band (0.5 %); no position falls inside the band and the calculation
oscillates between two positions. The dead band must be wider than the effect of one position.</p>
</details></div>
<div class="ex"><h4>Exercise 7 — Inverse-time curves</h4>
<p>An overcurrent relay has a threshold <i>I</i><sub>s</sub> = 200 A and TMS = 0.2. Compute its trip time (excluding breaker opening time) for a current of 1,000 A, then 2,000 A, with the SI (<i>k</i> = 0.14, α = 0.02), VI (13.5 and 1) and EI (80 and 2) curves. Which curve should you choose for a line where the fault current varies widely with fault location?</p>
<details class="sol"><summary>See the solution</summary>
<p><i>t</i> = TMS·<i>k</i>/((<i>I</i>/<i>I</i><sub>s</sub>)<sup>α</sup> − 1). At 1,000 A (<i>I</i>/<i>I</i><sub>s</sub> = 5): SI <span class="result">0.856 s</span>, VI <span class="result">0.675 s</span>, EI <span class="result">0.667 s</span>. At 2,000 A (ratio 10): SI 0.594 s, VI 0.300 s, EI 0.162 s. The SI curve is almost flat (0.86 s → 0.59 s); the EI curve collapses when the current doubles (0.67 s → 0.16 s). It therefore suits cases where the fault current varies widely with position (fast for a close fault, slow for a remote one, which makes grading easier) and where the protection must tolerate load energisation (downstream fuses); SI suits cases where the fault current varies little (strong network).</p>
</details></div>
<div class="ex"><h4>Exercise 8 — Compensated network</h4>
<p>A 20 kV, 50 Hz network has a total zero-sequence capacitance <i>C</i><sub>0</sub> = 8 µF. Compute the capacitive current <i>I</i><sub>c</sub> of a single-phase fault with isolated neutral and the voltage of the healthy phases. Compute the inductance of the tuned Petersen coil, then with a detuning <i>v</i> = +5 % and an active current of 3 % of <i>I</i><sub>c</sub>, and the residual fault current.</p>
<details class="sol"><summary>See the solution</summary>
<p><i>I</i><sub>c</sub> = 3·ω·<i>C</i><sub>0</sub>·<i>U</i><sub>n</sub>/√3 = 3 × 314.16 × 8·10⁻⁶ × 20,000/√3 = <span class="result">87.1 A</span>; the healthy phases rise to √3 × 11.55 kV = 20 kV (line-to-line voltage). Tuned coil: <i>L</i> = 1/(3·ω²·<i>C</i><sub>0</sub>) = <span class="result">0.422 H</span>. With <i>v</i> = +5 % (over-compensation): <i>L</i> = 1/(3·ω²·<i>C</i><sub>0</sub>·1.05) = <span class="result">0.402 H</span>; residual current ≈ <i>I</i><sub>c</sub>·√(<i>v</i>² + <i>r</i>²) = 87.1 × √(0.05² + 0.03²) = <span class="result">5.1 A</span>, 17 times less than with isolated neutral: the arc of a transient fault extinguishes by itself. Detuning avoids exact resonance (which would make the neutral voltage rise sharply); exact tuning is done by varying <i>L</i> until the neutral voltage peaks.</p>
</details></div>
<div class="ex"><h4>Exercise 9 — Current transformer saturation</h4>
<p>A 200/5 A, class 5P20, 15 VA CT has a secondary resistance <i>R</i><sub>ct</sub> = 0.3 Ω. It feeds a relay and leads with a total burden of 30 VA (at 5 A). Compute its actual accuracy limit factor ALF′. Does the CT remain accurate for a 3 kA fault? What should be done?</p>
<details class="sol"><summary>See the solution</summary>
<p>ALF′ = ALF·(<i>R</i><sub>ct</sub>·<i>I</i><sub>sn</sub>² + <i>S</i><sub>n</sub>)/(<i>R</i><sub>ct</sub>·<i>I</i><sub>sn</sub>² + <i>S</i><sub>b</sub>) = 20 × (0.3 × 25 + 15)/(0.3 × 25 + 30) = 20 × 22.5/37.5 = <span class="result">12</span>. The CT keeps its composite error up to 12 × 200 = 2,400 A. A 3,000 A fault, 15 times the rated current, exceeds this limit: the CT saturates, the relay sees a lower current and trips later. Solutions: reduce the burden (larger lead cross-section, low-burden relay) to restore ALF′ ≥ 15, or choose a CT with a higher rated burden (30 VA) or a higher ratio.</p>
</details></div>
<div class="ex"><h4>Exercise 10 — Motor start and voltage dip</h4>
<p>A 2 MVA motor starts direct-on-line with a starting current of 6 times its rated current, on a network with a short-circuit power of 40 MVA. Its starting torque is 1.2 pu (at rated voltage) and the pump it drives opposes a load torque of 0.5 pu at standstill. Estimate the voltage dip, then the accelerating torque at start. Does the motor start? What happens if the network is only 25 MVA?</p>
<details class="sol"><summary>See the solution</summary>
<p>Power drawn at start: <i>S</i><sub>d</sub> = 6 × 2 = 12 MVA; dip ≈ <i>S</i><sub>d</sub>/<i>S</i><sub>sc</sub> = 12/40 = <span class="result">30 %</span> (first order: refine it with a load flow, see example 5.5). Voltage falls to 0.70 pu; torque is 1.2 × 0.70² = <span class="result">0.59 pu</span>, an accelerating torque of 0.59 − 0.50 = 0.09 pu: the motor starts, but very slowly (current stays high for a long time and heats the motor). With 25 MVA: dip ≈ 48 %, voltage 0.52 pu, torque 1.2 × 0.52² = 0.32 pu &lt; 0.50: the motor <b>does not start</b>. Remedies: star-delta starter, soft starter, variable-speed drive, network reinforcement or reactive compensation.</p>
</details></div>
<div class="ex"><h4>Exercise 11 — Generator capability curve</h4>
<p>A 10 MVA generator (<i>X</i><sub>d</sub> = 1.8 pu, maximum excitation <i>E</i><sub>fd max</sub> = 2.5 pu) produces 8 MW. For a voltage of 1.00 pu and then 0.95 pu, compute the maximum reactive power it can supply, with the stator limit <i>p</i>² + <i>q</i>² ≤ <i>v</i>² and the excitation limit <i>p</i>² + (<i>q</i> + <i>v</i>²/<i>X</i><sub>d</sub>)² ≤ (<i>v</i>·<i>E</i><sub>fd max</sub>/<i>X</i><sub>d</sub>)². Which limit is reached first?</p>
<details class="sol"><summary>See the solution</summary>
<p><i>p</i> = 0.8 pu. At <i>v</i> = 1.00: stator <i>q</i> ≤ √(1 − 0.64) = 0.600 pu (6.0 Mvar); excitation <i>q</i> ≤ −1/1.8 + √((2.5/1.8)² − 0.64) = −0.556 + 1.136 = 0.580 pu (<span class="result">5.80 Mvar</span>): <b>excitation</b> is the limit. At <i>v</i> = 0.95: stator <i>q</i> ≤ √(0.9025 − 0.64) = 0.512 pu (<span class="result">5.12 Mvar</span>); excitation <i>q</i> ≤ −0.9025/1.8 + √((0.95 × 2.5/1.8)² − 0.64) = 0.548 pu: now the <b>stator current</b> is the limit. A low voltage therefore reduces reactive capability just when the network needs it most; in the load flow, the PV bus becomes PQ as soon as the requested reactive power exceeds this limit and the voltage is no longer held (example 2.14).</p>
</details></div>
<div class="ex"><h4>Exercise 12 — Distance protection of a line</h4>
<p>A 10 km line has an impedance of 0.3 + j0.4 Ω/km. A distance relay is set with the standard zones (80 % instantaneous, 120 % at 0.3 s, 200 % at 1 s). Compute the reaches in ohms. Which zone sees a solid fault at 6 km, 9 km, 11 km (on the next line)? What is the effect, on an earth fault, of a parallel line coupled in zero sequence?</p>
<details class="sol"><summary>See the solution</summary>
<p><i>Z</i><sub>line</sub> = 10 × (0.3 + j0.4) = 3 + j4 Ω, |<i>Z</i>| = 5 Ω. Reaches: zone 1 = <span class="result">4 Ω</span>, zone 2 = <span class="result">6 Ω</span>, zone 3 = <span class="result">10 Ω</span>. Fault at 6 km: <i>Z</i> = 3 Ω &lt; 4 Ω, zone 1, instantaneous trip. At 9 km: 4.5 Ω, between 4 and 6 Ω, zone 2, trip at 0.3 s (the end of the line is not cleared instantly; a teleprotection scheme or a differential protection would fill this gap). At 11 km (beyond the line): 5.5 Ω, seen in zone 2: the relay is then back-up for the next line, delayed by 0.3 s: its own protection must act first. A parallel line coupled in zero sequence drives an extra residual current in the protected line which distorts the earth-fault impedance measurement: the relay under- or over-estimates the distance depending on current direction, which requires reducing zone 1 (70 % instead of 80 %) or compensating with the parallel line current.</p>
</details></div>
<div class="ex"><h4>Exercise 13 — Mutual coupling of two parallel lines</h4>
<p>Two identical lines connect a source substation to a 20 kV delivery substation in parallel (<i>U</i><sub>n</sub> = 20 kV, <i>c</i> = 1.1). Each has a zero-sequence impedance <i>Z</i><sub>0</sub> = 6 + j18 Ω and a zero-sequence mutual impedance <i>Z</i><sub>0m</sub> = 2 + j8 Ω (currents in the same direction). Seen from the delivery substation, the positive-sequence impedance (upstream network and the lines in parallel) is <i>Z</i><sub>1</sub> = <i>Z</i><sub>2</sub> = 2 + j6 Ω. Calculate the single-phase earth-fault current <i>I</i>″<sub>k1</sub> at the delivery substation, without then with the coupling. Does the three-phase fault change?</p>
<details class="sol"><summary>Show solution</summary>
<p>Without coupling, the two lines in parallel give <i>Z</i><sub>0</sub>/2 = 3 + j9 Ω; <i>Z</i><sub>1</sub> + <i>Z</i><sub>2</sub> + <i>Z</i><sub>0</sub> = 7 + j21 Ω, modulus 22.1 Ω: <i>I</i>″<sub>k1</sub> = √3 × 1.1 × 20,000/22.1 = <span class="result">1,721 A</span>. With coupling: <i>Z</i><sub>0,eq</sub> = (<i>Z</i><sub>0</sub> + <i>Z</i><sub>0m</sub>)/2 = 4 + j13 Ω; sum 8 + j25 Ω, modulus 26.2 Ω: <i>I</i>″<sub>k1</sub> = <span class="result">1,452 A</span>, i.e. −16 %. The three-phase fault, <i>I</i>″<sub>k3</sub> = 1.1 × 20,000/(√3 × 6.32) = 2,008 A, does not change: in positive sequence the three currents cancel and the mutual has no effect. Practical consequence: a distance relay that neglects the mutual misjudges the distance of an earth fault, hence the earth compensation factor.</p>
</details></div>
<div class="ex"><h4>Exercise 14 — Choosing and placing a surge arrester</h4>
<p>A 20 kV network (<i>U</i><sub>m</sub> = 24 kV) supplies a transformer with a lightning impulse withstand BIL = 125 kV. The chosen arrester has a residual voltage <i>U</i><sub>pl</sub> = 65 kV; the lightning wave has a steepness <i>S</i> = 1,000 kV/µs and a margin of 1.2 on the BIL is required. (1) What is the maximum distance between arrester and transformer? (2) Which rated voltage <i>U</i><sub>r</sub> should be chosen (among 18, 21, 24, 27 kV, with a temporary overvoltage withstand factor <i>T</i> = 1.05) with an isolated neutral (<i>k</i> = √3), then with a solidly earthed neutral (<i>k</i> = 1.4)?</p>
<details class="sol"><summary>Show solution</summary>
<p>(1) The voltage at the transformer is <i>U</i><sub>eq</sub> = <i>U</i><sub>pl</sub> + 2·<i>S</i>·<i>L</i>/<i>v</i> (<i>v</i> = 300 m/µs) and must stay ≤ 125/1.2 = 104.2 kV. So 2 × 1,000 × <i>L</i>/300 ≤ 39.2, i.e. <i>L</i> ≤ <span class="result">5.9 m</span>: the arrester must be as close as possible to the transformer. (2) Temporary overvoltage <i>k</i>·<i>U</i><sub>m</sub>/√3. Isolated neutral: √3 × 24/√3 = 24 kV, so <i>U</i><sub>r</sub> ≥ 24/1.05 = 22.9 kV: <span class="result">24 kV</span>. Solidly earthed: 1.4 × 24/√3 = 19.4 kV, so <i>U</i><sub>r</sub> ≥ 18.5 kV: <span class="result">21 kV</span> (18 kV is not enough). An arrester with a higher rated voltage has a higher residual voltage: re-check the margin of question (1) with the <i>U</i><sub>pl</sub> of the chosen model.</p>
</details></div>

