        <div class="section" id="n2-qcm">
            <h2><span class="section-icon">📋</span>Level 2 quiz</h2>
<div class="qcm" data-answer="b"><p class="q">1. In the π model of a line, the total capacitance C is distributed:</p>
<ol><li data-k="a">entirely at the sending end</li><li data-k="b">C/2 at each end</li><li data-k="c">in series with R</li><li data-k="d">C at each end</li></ol>
<div class="fb">C/2 on each side: this is what NRJXpert does (corrected in October 2026: each side used to be C·l).</div></div>
<div class="qcm" data-answer="c"><p class="q">2. A transformer has u<sub>sc</sub> = 4 %. Its impedance on its own base is:</p>
<ol><li data-k="a">4 Ω</li><li data-k="b">0.4 pu</li><li data-k="c">0.04 pu</li><li data-k="d">25 pu</li></ol>
<div class="fb">u<sub>sc</sub> is the impedance in pu on the base (S<sub>n</sub>, U<sub>n</sub>).</div></div>
<div class="qcm" data-answer="a"><p class="q">3. On a highly resistive low-voltage network, the voltage drop depends mainly on:</p>
<ol><li data-k="a">the active power</li><li data-k="b">the reactive power</li><li data-k="c">the frequency</li><li data-k="d">the cable capacitance</li></ol>
<div class="fb">ΔU/U ≈ (RP + XQ)/U² with R ≫ X.</div></div>
<div class="qcm" data-answer="d"><p class="q">4. In a load flow, the unknowns of a PV node are:</p>
<ol><li data-k="a">P and Q</li><li data-k="b">|V| and θ</li><li data-k="c">P and θ</li><li data-k="d">Q and θ</li></ol>
<div class="fb">P and |V| are imposed; Q and the angle are calculated.</div></div>
<div class="qcm" data-answer="b"><p class="q">5. The slack node is used to:</p>
<ol><li data-k="a">set the frequency</li><li data-k="b">provide the angle reference and cover the unknown losses</li><li data-k="c">represent the loads</li><li data-k="d">control the reactive power of the PV nodes</li></ol>
<div class="fb">Since losses are known only after the calculation, one node must balance the power.</div></div>
<div class="qcm" data-answer="c"><p class="q">6. The fast decoupled method is poorly suited to:</p>
<ol><li data-k="a">large transmission networks</li><li data-k="b">meshed networks</li><li data-k="c">distribution networks with high R/X</li><li data-k="d">networks without a PV node</li></ol>
<div class="fb">It assumes R ≪ X; in distribution the backward/forward sweep is preferred (level 5).</div></div>
<div class="qcm" data-answer="a"><p class="q">7. To size the breaking capacity of a circuit breaker, one uses:</p>
<ol><li data-k="a">the maximum I"k (c<sub>max</sub>)</li><li data-k="b">the minimum I"k (c<sub>min</sub>)</li><li data-k="c">the rated current</li><li data-k="d">the overload current</li></ol>
<div class="fb">The minimum current is used for the setting (sensitivity) of the protection.</div></div>
<div class="qcm" data-answer="d"><p class="q">8. A phase-to-earth fault connects the sequence networks:</p>
<ol><li data-k="a">positive only</li><li data-k="b">positive and negative in opposition</li><li data-k="c">negative and zero in parallel</li><li data-k="d">positive, negative and zero in series</li></ol>
<div class="fb">I"k1 = √3·c·Un/|Z1 + Z2 + Z0|.</div></div>
<div class="qcm" data-answer="b"><p class="q">9. A delta winding:</p>
<ol><li data-k="a">lets zero-sequence current through to the upstream network</li><li data-k="b">blocks the zero-sequence current</li><li data-k="c">doubles the positive-sequence impedance</li><li data-k="d">suppresses the three-phase fault current</li></ol>
<div class="fb">The zero-sequence current circulates in the delta without leaving on the lines.</div></div>
<div class="qcm" data-answer="c"><p class="q">10. For R/X = 0.1, the factor κ is about:</p>
<ol><li data-k="a">1.02</li><li data-k="b">1.30</li><li data-k="c">1.75</li><li data-k="d">2.00</li></ol>
<div class="fb">κ = 1.02 + 0.98·e<sup>−0.3</sup> = 1.746: highly inductive network, large DC component.</div></div>
<div class="qcm" data-answer="a"><p class="q">11. Why are the curves of overcurrent protections inverse-time?</p>
<ol><li data-k="a">so that trip time decreases as current increases, like the <i>I</i>²·<i>t</i> heating that must be limited</li><li data-k="b">to trip faster the lower the current is</li><li data-k="c">so that time is the same whatever the current</li><li data-k="d">to avoid needing a circuit breaker</li></ol>
<div class="fb">Heating is proportional to I²·t: a moderate overload is tolerated for a long time, a short circuit is cleared quickly.</div></div>
<div class="qcm" data-answer="c"><p class="q">12. Two overcurrent protections in series are selective (time grading) if:</p>
<ol><li data-k="a">their curves cross</li><li data-k="b">they have the same TMS</li><li data-k="c">their trip times differ by at least the margin (≈ 0.3 s) for every possible fault current</li><li data-k="d">the upstream protection is instantaneous</li></ol>
<div class="fb">Curves that cross mean that above some current the upstream device trips before the downstream one: selectivity is lost.</div></div>
<div class="qcm" data-answer="d"><p class="q">13. The threshold <i>I</i><sub>s</sub> of an overcurrent protection must be:</p>
<ol><li data-k="a">below the load current, to miss nothing</li><li data-k="b">equal to the maximum short-circuit current</li><li data-k="c">independent of the load current</li><li data-k="d">above the maximum load current and well below the minimum short-circuit current</li></ol>
<div class="fb">Too low: nuisance trips; too high: the furthest fault (Isc min) is not seen.</div></div>
<div class="qcm" data-answer="b"><p class="q">14. A distance relay measures:</p>
<ol><li data-k="a">current only</li><li data-k="b">the impedance V/I, proportional to the distance to the fault</li><li data-k="c">voltage only</li><li data-k="d">the network frequency</li></ol>
<div class="fb">A line's impedance is proportional to its length: V/I gives the distance whatever the current magnitude.</div></div>
<div class="qcm" data-answer="c"><p class="q">15. Compared with a circuit breaker with a relay, a fuse:</p>
<ol><li data-k="a">can be reclosed automatically</li><li data-k="b">only protects against overloads</li><li data-k="c">clears before the short-circuit peak but must be replaced after operating</li><li data-k="d">needs a CT to work</li></ol>
<div class="fb">The fuse combines sensor, relay and interruption; it limits the peak current but is single-use.</div></div>
<div class="qcm" data-answer="a"><p class="q">16. A 5P20 protection CT is loaded more heavily than its rated burden. Its actual limit factor:</p>
<ol><li data-k="a">decreases: it saturates at a lower current and the relay trips later</li><li data-k="b">stays equal to 20</li><li data-k="c">increases, because the secondary voltage rises</li><li data-k="d">no longer matters</li></ol>
<div class="fb">ALF′ = ALF·(R<sub>ct</sub>·I<sub>sn</sub>² + S<sub>n</sub>)/(R<sub>ct</sub>·I<sub>sn</sub>² + S<sub>b</sub>): the larger S<sub>b</sub>, the smaller ALF′.</div></div>
<div class="qcm" data-answer="d"><p class="q">17. With an isolated neutral, a single-phase fault:</p>
<ol><li data-k="a">gives a current of several kA, as with solid earthing</li><li data-k="b">leaves the healthy-phase voltage unchanged</li><li data-k="c">requires instantaneous tripping</li><li data-k="d">closes only through the zero-sequence capacitances; healthy phases rise to √3 times the phase voltage</li></ol>
<div class="fb">I<sub>c</sub> = 3·ω·C<sub>0</sub>·U<sub>n</sub>/√3: a few amperes to a few hundred amperes; operation may continue.</div></div>
<div class="qcm" data-answer="b"><p class="q">18. A tuned Petersen coil:</p>
<ol><li data-k="a">increases the fault current</li><li data-k="b">compensates the capacitive fault current (L = 1/(3·ω²·C<sub>0</sub>)): only a small residual current remains</li><li data-k="c">connects the neutral solidly to earth</li><li data-k="d">replaces the substation transformer</li></ol>
<div class="fb">The residual current ≈ I<sub>c</sub>·√(v² + r²) is a few percent of I<sub>c</sub>: the transient arc extinguishes by itself.</div></div>
<div class="qcm" data-answer="a"><p class="q">19. An arrester is properly coordinated with a transformer if:</p>
<ol><li data-k="a">its equivalent voltage Ueq stays at least 20 % below the lightning impulse withstand (BIL) and its Ur covers the temporary overvoltage</li><li data-k="b">its residual voltage equals the BIL</li><li data-k="c">its Ur is as low as possible</li><li data-k="d">the distance to the transformer has no effect</li></ol>
<div class="fb">Ueq = Upl + 2·S·L/v increases with distance L: a too-distant arrester no longer protects.</div></div>
<div class="qcm" data-answer="c"><p class="q">20. At direct-on-line start, an induction motor:</p>
<ol><li data-k="a">draws its rated current at a good power factor</li><li data-k="b">develops a torque independent of voltage</li><li data-k="c">draws 5 to 7 times its rated current at a low power factor, and its torque varies as V²</li><li data-k="d">supplies reactive power to the network</li></ol>
<div class="fb">The voltage dip reduces torque (80 % V → 64 % torque): if the load torque is larger, the motor does not start or stalls.</div></div>
<div class="qcm" data-answer="d"><p class="q">21. The mutual coupling of two parallel lines acts:</p>
<ol><li data-k="a">in all three sequence networks</li><li data-k="b">only in the negative-sequence network</li><li data-k="c">only for three-phase faults</li><li data-k="d">only in the zero-sequence network (currents in phase, return through earth)</li></ol>
<div class="fb">In positive and negative sequence the three phases cancel; in zero sequence, Z<sub>0m</sub> reaches 40 to 70 % of Z<sub>0</sub>.</div></div>
<div class="qcm" data-answer="b"><p class="q">22. In the load flow, a generator whose requested reactive power exceeds its excitation limit:</p>
<ol><li data-k="a">stays a PV bus and supplies more reactive power</li><li data-k="b">becomes a PQ bus with Q equal to the limit, and its voltage is no longer held</li><li data-k="c">always makes the calculation diverge</li><li data-k="d">becomes the slack bus</li></ol>
<div class="fb">The capability curve bounds Q; the bus returns to PV if the voltage crosses back to the right side of the set-point.</div></div>
